// Weather glyphs — inline SVG bodies for WeatherIcon.
//
// Redrawn from Microsoft's Fluent emoji set, which these icons were previously
// loaded from over jsDelivr as <img>. An external SVG in an <img> is a sealed
// document: it can't be animated, can't follow the theme, and can't be reached
// into (which is why StormStrongIcon used to overlay a hand-copied bolt, and why
// fog was always drawn inline). Inlining them removes the CDN round-trip too,
// which matters for the offline PWA and the APK shell.
//
// Two deliberate reductions from the upstream art:
//   1. Every feGaussianBlur inner-shadow filter is dropped. They read as a faint
//      bevel at 200px and as nothing at all at the 22px the hourly strip uses,
//      and filters are the expensive part of rendering an SVG.
//   2. Fluent stacks up to ten gradient passes over one path (the sun does; the
//      rain cloud does it five times). Only the base fill and the one or two
//      passes that visibly shape the form are kept.
// Between them the set went from ~230 KB of downloaded SVG to what's below.
//
// Cloud body colours are CSS variables, not hexes, so dark mode can tone down
// the pale lavender instead of glowing against a dark ground. See --wi-cloud*
// in App.css.
//
// Each glyph takes `u`, a per-instance id prefix from useId(). Gradient ids are
// document-global, so without it two icons on the same screen would collide.
//
// Generated from the upstream assets rather than transcribed by hand; edit the
// shapes here, not upstream.

